-- title:
--   Transport of the q-expansion fields of X₀(M), X₁(M) along τ
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and $M \ge 1$. Let $\kappa$ and $k$ be fields of characteristic $p$ with $k$ algebraically closed and such that every $x \in k$ satisfies $x^{p^n} = x$ for some $n > 0$, and let $\tau : \kappa \to k$ be a ring homomorphism. Inside $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,K$ write $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,K\,M$ for the intermediate field generated over $K$ by the two series $\mathrm{jqModC}\,K = q^{-1}\cdot(\text{image of the integral power series }\mathrm{jNum})$ and its $q \mapsto q^{M}$ substitution $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,K\,M$, and $\mathrm{x1FunctionFieldC}\,K\,M$ for the intermediate field generated over $K$ by the set `intFormRatiosC K (Gamma1 M)`. Assume the first field is contained in the second over $\kappa$, and that for the resulting algebra structure the larger is finite-dimensional and separable over the smaller. Then there are ring homomorphisms $L_\theta$, $L_\varphi$ from the two fields over $\kappa$ to the corresponding fields over $k$, and an inclusion of the two fields over $k$, such that: on underlying Laurent series both maps act by applying $\tau$ to all coefficients (`coeffMap τ`); both send the constant $c \in \kappa$ to the constant $\tau(c)$; $L_\theta$ carries the elements $\mathrm{jGeomGen}$ and $\mathrm{jNGeomGen}$ over $\kappa$ (namely $\mathrm{jqModC}$ and $\mathrm{jqNModC}$) to their counterparts over $k$; the $k$-subalgebra generated by the range of each of $L_\theta$, $L_\varphi$ is everything; and over $k$ the larger field is again finite-dimensional and separable over the smaller.
--
--   This is the constant-field transport step for the $q$-expansion function fields of $X_0(M)$ and $X_1(M)$ in characteristic $p$: coefficientwise application of $\tau$ moves the whole configuration (inclusion, generators $j$ and $j_M$, finiteness and separability) from $\kappa$ to an algebraically closed $k$ algebraic over $\mathbb{F}_p$, with generation over the new constant field. It is used in the computation of the cardinality of inertia groups at the cusps of the special fibre of the level-$M$ modular curve, where the argument may be run over an algebraically closed base.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_exists_ringHom_modularFunctionFieldC_x1FunctionFieldC_coe_eq_coeffMap_of_forall_exists_pow_eq.lean

